.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt NIMZO 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nimzo
.Nd dwl-style Wayland compositor in Nim
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl s Ar command
.Op Fl c Ar config
.Op Fl C
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
uses wlroots to provide a Wayland compositor with a zones layout.
Reads runtime output, keyboard, and colour settings from
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nimzo/config
(or
.Pa ~/.config/nimzo/config ),
then
.Pa /etc/nimzo/config .
If no file exists, built-in dwl defaults apply.
.Fl c
selects an explicit configuration file.
.Fl C
validates configuration and exits without starting a Wayland backend.
Other policy and input settings remain in
.Pa config_defaults.nim
and require a rebuild.
.Fl d
turns on debug logging.
.Fl v
prints the version.
.Fl s
starts a command with
.Pa /bin/sh -c .
At shutdown, nimzo sends SIGTERM to the startup process group and waits for it.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
The configuration file uses a supported subset of sway syntax.
It replaces the complete keyboard binding set, even if it contains no bindings.
There are no implicit keyboard bindings when a file is loaded.
Other settings start with their runtime defaults.
Unknown directives, unsupported commands, and invalid values are errors.
This is not the full sway configuration language: include files, arbitrary
command blocks, binding modes, and workspace-to-output assignments are not supported.
.Pp
Check a file without starting a Wayland backend:
.Dl nimzo -C -c ~/.config/nimzo/config
This checks configuration and command syntax, not output hardware capabilities.
Load an alternate file with:
.Dl nimzo -c /path/to/config
Use a
.Cm reload
binding or
.Ql swaymsg reload
to reload the selected file.
Syntax or command errors leave the previous configuration active.
Reload updates bindings, colours, fonts, pointer modifiers, and configured outputs.
It preserves per-window titlebar choices and fixed-size state.
It preserves session layouts unless the configured default layout changes.
Startup commands and new-window rules do not run again.
.Ss Syntax and variables
Write one directive per line.
Blank lines and comments are ignored.
A comment starts with an unquoted
.Ql #
at the start of a line or after whitespace.
A colour token such as
.Ql #005577
is not a comment after a directive.
Use single or double quotes for arguments that contain spaces.
Backslashes escape characters; there is no multiline continuation syntax.
Only output properties use brace-delimited blocks.
Put the opening brace after the output name and the closing brace on its own line.
.Pp
Configuration variables use
.Ql $name
syntax.
Names start with a letter or underscore and can then contain letters, digits,
or underscores.
Variables are expanded in declaration order, including inside quoted text.
Unknown variables remain unchanged; shell commands can therefore use variables
such as
.Ev HOME .
Configuration expansion is textual and is not shell evaluation.
Use an earlier variable definition before any line that needs its value.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm set Ar $name value
Define or replace a configuration variable.
A value can contain several words.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
set $mod Mod4
set $term foot
set $menu "bemenu-run"
set $accent #005577
set $launcher $menu
.Ed
.El
.Ss Keyboard and pointer configuration
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm bindsym Ar CHORD COMMAND Ns Op , Ar COMMAND Ns ...
Bind an XKB keysym to one or more commands from
.Sx Binding commands .
A chord is
.Ar MODIFIER Ns + Ns ... Ns + Ns Ar KEYSYM ,
or just a keysym for an unmodified binding.
Modifier names are case-insensitive:
.Cm Shift ,
.Cm Ctrl Ns / Ns Cm Control ,
.Cm Alt Ns / Ns Cm Mod1 ,
.Cm Super Ns / Ns Cm Logo Ns / Ns Cm Mod4 ,
.Cm Mod3 ,
and
.Cm Mod5 .
.Cm None
specifies no modifier and cannot be combined with another modifier.
Use XKB names such as
.Cm Return ,
.Cm space ,
.Cm Backslash ,
.Cm KP_Left ,
and
.Cm XF86AudioRaiseVolume .
Keypad bindings also work with Num Lock enabled.
The last binding for the same keysym and modifiers wins.
Pointer-button names and sway binding flags such as
.Fl -release
are not supported.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Return exec $term
bindsym $mod+Shift+r reload
bindsym XF86AudioRaiseVolume exec volctl up
.Ed
.It Cm floating_modifier Ar MODIFIER Ns Op + Ns Ar MODIFIER Ns ...
Select the modifier for left-button move, right-button resize,
middle-button floating toggle, and vertical workspace scrolling.
The default is
.Cm Super .
Use the same modifier names as
.Cm bindsym ,
or
.Cm none
for no modifier.
Pointer actions are fixed and are not assigned with
.Cm bindsym .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
floating_modifier $mod
.Ed
.El
.Ss Appearance
Colours use
.Ar #RRGGBB
or
.Ar #RRGGBBAA
hexadecimal notation; the last two digits specify alpha.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm client.background Ar COLOUR
Set the desktop background colour.
The default is
.Ql #222222 .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
client.background #222222
.Ed
.It Cm fullscreen_background Ar COLOUR
Set the fullscreen backdrop colour.
The default is black.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fullscreen_background #000000
.Ed
.It Cm client.focused Ar COLOURS
Set the focused window's decoration colours.
.Ar COLOURS
is one colour or three to six colours in this order:
.Ar border background text
.Op Ar indicator
.Op Ar child_border
.Op Ar titlebar_border .
With one colour, background and outlines use that colour and text is white.
With three or more colours, omitted fields use the border colour.
The sixth field sets the titlebar outline.
Indicator and child-border fields are accepted for compatibility; they do not
create extra decoration elements.
The default focused border is
.Ql #005577 .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
client.focused #005577 #005577 #ffffff #005577 #005577 #0088aa
.Ed
.It Cm client.unfocused Ar COLOURS
Set unfocused decoration colours, with the same fields as
.Cm client.focused .
The default border is
.Ql #444444 .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
client.unfocused #444444 #333333 #dddddd
.Ed
.It Cm client.urgent Ar COLOURS
Set urgent decoration colours, with the same fields as
.Cm client.focused .
The default border is red.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
client.urgent #ff0000
.Ed
.It Cm font Oo Cm pango: Oc Ns Ar DESCRIPTION
Select a Pango font description.
The optional
.Ql pango:
prefix is accepted.
The default is
.Ql monospace 10 .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
font pango:monospace 10
.Ed
.It Cm titlebars Ar on|off
Set the initial titlebar policy for new windows.
The default is
.Cm off .
This does not change existing windows on reload; use the singular
.Cm titlebar
binding command for the focused window.
Bars are 24 logical pixels high, show literal text, and use end ellipsis.
Windows with a missing or empty title keep the normal border instead.
If the window supplies a title later, the bar returns if enabled.
Fullscreen hides the frame without changing the window's titlebar choice.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
titlebars on
.Ed
.It Cm window_stack_picker Ar on|off
Show a titlebar badge and picker for covered windows.
The default is
.Cm off .
Changes take effect on reload.
.El
.Ss Layout and zones
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm layout Ar monocle|zones
Select the default layout for outputs.
.Cm monocle
fills the usable area with each tiled window;
.Cm zones
is the default and uses named rectangles.
If the configuration defines no zones, nimzo uses two equal, full-height
zones:
.Cm L
on the left and
.Cm R
on the right.
Explicit zone definitions replace this built-in set.
At top level,
.Cm layout toggle
is not accepted; it is a binding command.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
layout zones
zone L 0% 0% 50% 100%
zone R 50% 0% 50% 100%
.Ed
.It Cm zone Ar NAME X% Y% WIDTH% HEIGHT%
Define a named rectangle as percentages of each output's usable area,
after panel reservations.
Values must be finite and nonnegative, width and height must be positive,
and the rectangle must fit within 0 to 100 percent.
Zones can overlap.
A name is 1 to 63 bytes and cannot contain whitespace or
.Ql | .
A later definition with the same name replaces the earlier one.
Without
.Cm default_zone ,
new normal windows prefer an unoccupied zone with similar dimensions.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zone C 12.5% 12.5% 75% 75%
.Ed
.It Cm default_zone Ar NAME
Place new windows that use automatic zone placement in this zone, even if
it already contains a window.
The name must match a built-in or configured zone.
An explicit window rule can choose a different zone.
Floating and always-on-top windows keep their normal placement behavior.
Reloading this setting does not move windows already assigned to a zone.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
default_zone C
.Ed
.It Cm snap_distance Ar PIXELS
Set mouse move and resize edge snapping in logical pixels.
The value is a nonnegative integer; the default is 32.
Zero disables snapping.
Output edges and, in zones layout, zone edges participate.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
snap_distance 24
.Ed
.El
.Ss Output configuration
Use
.Cm output Ar NAME PROPERTY Op Ar ARGUMENTS
for one property, or use a block:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output DP-3 {
    default
    mode 2560x1440@60Hz
    position 0 0
    scale 1
    transform normal
    enable
}
output HDMI-A-1 position below DP-3 gap 1
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar NAME
is an exact connector name or
.Ql * .
Partial wildcard names are not supported.
Use
.Ql wlr-randr
or
.Ql swaymsg -t get_outputs
inside a running session to find connector names.
Wildcard properties apply before connector-specific properties, regardless of
their declaration order.
Repeated declarations for an output merge; the last value of a property wins.
Unspecified properties keep their current values on reload.
Output changes are not atomic across outputs; hardware rejection is reported
on standard error.
Do not run another persistent output configurator with these rules.
.Pp
Each property below can appear after
.Cm output Ar NAME
or on its own line inside an output block.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm mode Ar WIDTHxHEIGHT Ns Op @ Ns Ar HZ Ns Op Cm Hz
Select positive pixel dimensions and an optional refresh rate in hertz.
Refresh rates can be fractional; zero or an omitted rate leaves rate selection
to the backend.
.Cm resolution
is an alias.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output DP-3 mode 2560x1440@59.95Hz
output HDMI-A-1 resolution 1920x1080
.Ed
.It Cm position Ar X Y
Place an output at signed integer coordinates in the logical desktop.
.Cm pos
is an alias, and
.Ar X,Y
notation is also accepted.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output DP-3 position 0 0
output HDMI-A-1 pos 0,1441
.Ed
.It Cm position below Ar OUTPUT Op Cm gap Ar PIXELS
Left-align this output below an exact anchor output, using the anchor's
logical height after scale and rotation.
The gap is a nonnegative integer and defaults to zero.
Both names must be distinct exact connector names, not wildcards.
Relative-position cycles are errors.
When an anchor is absent or disabled, current or automatic placement remains
in effect.
Placement is recalculated on reload and output connection.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output HDMI-A-1 position below DP-3 gap 1
.Ed
.It Cm scale Ar FACTOR
Set a finite positive output scale.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output DP-3 scale 1.5
.Ed
.It Cm transform Ar TRANSFORM
Select
.Cm normal ,
.Cm 90 ,
.Cm 180 ,
.Cm 270 ,
.Cm flipped ,
.Cm flipped-90 ,
.Cm flipped-180 ,
or
.Cm flipped-270 .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output HDMI-A-1 transform 90
.Ed
.It Cm enable
Enable the matching output.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output HDMI-A-1 enable
.Ed
.It Cm disable
Disable the matching output.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output HDMI-A-1 disable
.Ed
.It Cm default
Choose the initial focused output if it is connected and enabled.
Only one exact connector can be marked default.
Changing this property on reload does not change focus or move workspaces.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
output DP-3 default
.Ed
.El
.Pp
If an output is removed or disabled, nimzo moves its windows to an active
output, or to a private headless output if none is active.
When the original output returns, nimzo restores saved tags, zone assignment,
and window geometry unless the user changed the window in the meantime.
.Ss Startup programs
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm exec Ar SHELL_COMMAND
Run a command through
.Pa /bin/sh -c
once after the Wayland and IPC sockets are ready.
This top-level directive does not run again on reload.
Each
.Cm exec
line starts an independent process.
Unlike the command supplied with
.Fl s ,
these processes do not form a supervised startup process group.
.It Cm wayland_client Ar NAME Ar CAPABILITY Ns Op + Ns Ar CAPABILITY ... Ar /PATH Op Ar ARG ...
Define a private Wayland launch profile.
Use an absolute executable path.
Arguments are fixed by the configuration; quote arguments that contain spaces.
Nimzo does not search
.Ev PATH
or run a shell.
See
.Sx WAYLAND CAPABILITIES .
.It Cm launch Ar NAME
Start the named profile once at startup.
Like top-level
.Cm exec ,
it does not run again on reload.
.El
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wayland_client waybar toplevel /usr/bin/waybar
launch waybar
exec swaybg -i "$HOME/Pictures/wallpaper.png" -m fill
.Ed
.Ss New-window rules
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm for_window Li [ Ar CRITERIA Li ] Ar COMMAND Ns Op , Ar COMMAND Ns ...
Apply commands to each newly mapped managed window that matches the criteria.
The square brackets are required.
Use one or more whitespace-separated
.Ar key Ns = Ns Ar REGEX
criteria.
Supported keys are
.Cm app_id ,
.Cm title ,
.Cm class ,
and
.Cm instance .
The last two apply only to Xwayland windows;
.Cm app_id
also matches the Xwayland class for compatibility.
Regular expressions are case-sensitive and can match a substring.
Use
.Ql ^
and
.Ql $
anchors for an exact match.
All criteria in one rule must match.
.Pp
All matching rules run in declaration order.
Commands target the matched window, not the focused window.
Rules do not run on title changes or reload; reopen a window to apply new rules.
Supported rule commands are
.Cm fixedSize ,
.Cm titlebar ,
.Cm border ,
.Cm floating ,
.Cm alwaysOnTop ,
.Cm fullscreen ,
and
.Cm maximize
with
.Ar on|off|toggle ;
.Cm minimize ;
.Cm kill ;
.Cm move container to zone ;
.Cm move container to workspace ;
.Cm move container to output ;
and
.Cm exec .
Their syntax is defined under
.Sx Binding commands .
Other binding commands are not valid in rules.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
for_window [app_id="^org[.]example[.]Dialog$"] fixedSize on, titlebar off
for_window [class="^Pianoteq 9$"] fixedSize on
for_window [class="^Example$" instance="^utility$"] floating on
for_window [app_id="^foot$" title="^Monitor$"] move container to zone R
for_window [app_id="^org[.]example[.]Mail$"] move container to workspace 3
.Ed
.El
.Ss Binding commands
The commands below are accepted after
.Cm bindsym
and through the supported Sway IPC interface.
They are not top-level directives unless explicitly described above.
Commands that act on a window normally target the focused managed window.
In a
.Cm for_window
rule, supported window commands target the matching new window instead.
.Pp
Separate commands with commas outside quotes.
.Cm exec
consumes the rest of the line as shell text, including commas and semicolons.
Put it last if a binding must first run another compositor command.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+3 move container to workspace 3, workspace 3
bindsym $mod+Return workspace 1, exec $term
.Ed
.Pp
In the syntax below,
.Ar TAG
is an integer from 1 to 9 or
.Cm all ;
.Ar DIRECTION
is
.Cm left ,
.Cm right ,
.Cm up ,
or
.Cm down .
Tags are local to each output.
Relative workspace commands include empty tags and wrap between 1 and 9.
With several visible tags, the lowest visible tag is the navigation anchor.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm exec Ar SHELL_COMMAND
Start a shell command each time the binding is activated.
This differs from top-level startup
.Cm exec .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+p exec bemenu-run
.Ed
.It Cm launch Ar NAME
Start the named Wayland profile with its configured capabilities and arguments.
Use this command in a binding or over trusted Sway IPC, not in a window rule.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wayland_client waylock session-lock /usr/bin/waylock
bindsym Ctrl+Alt+l launch waylock
.Ed
.It Cm reload
Reload the selected configuration file.
With no selected file, report that there is no file to reload.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+r reload
.Ed
.It Cm exit
End the compositor session.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Escape exit
.Ed
.It Cm kill
Request that the focused window close.
This is a polite protocol request, not a forced process kill; the application
can show an unsaved-changes prompt.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+w kill
.Ed
.It Cm focus Ar next|prev
Cycle through windows on the focused output's selected tags, including
minimized and fullscreen windows.
While the binding modifiers are held, a minimized window is restored as a preview.
Cycling past it minimizes it again; releasing a binding modifier keeps the
selected window restored.
Fullscreen state and restore geometry are retained.
Window order is stable while focus changes.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Tab focus next
bindsym $mod+Shift+Tab focus prev
.Ed
.It Cm focus last
Focus the most recently focused eligible window other than the current window.
Repeat the command to switch back.
Only visible windows on the focused output are eligible.
The command skips minimized and always-on-top windows.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+grave focus last
.Ed
.It Cm focus output Op Ar DIRECTION|next|NAME
Focus an output and move the pointer to the centre of its usable area.
With no argument or with
.Cm next ,
cycle through enabled outputs ordered by logical Y, then X, then connector name.
Cycling wraps and skips disabled outputs.
A name selects an exact enabled connector.
Directions select a neighbouring output, with wraparound at an edge.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+o focus output next
bindsym $mod+KP_Add focus output down
bindsym $mod+F2 focus output DP-3
.Ed
.It Cm swap outputs Op Ar NAME Op Ar NAME
With no names, rotate each enabled output's complete contents to the next
output in the same order as
.Cm focus output next .
Two outputs swap.
One name swaps the focused output with that connector; two names select a pair.
All managed windows move, including hidden-tag windows.
Tag masks, view history, both layout slots, zone assignments,
and fixed/floating/fullscreen state are retained.
Floating and restore positions are translated to the new usable origin.
Focus stays on the same physical output.
Modes, scaling, panel reservations, and output identities do not move.
Unknown or disabled connector names are errors.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+o swap outputs
bindsym $mod+F3 swap outputs HDMI-A-1
bindsym $mod+F4 swap outputs DP-3 HDMI-A-1
.Ed
.It Cm move container to output Ar DIRECTION
Move the focused window to an output in the given direction.
Assign the destination's selected tags.
Chain an output-focus command to follow it.
This command accepts a direction, not a connector name.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+KP_Add move container to output down, focus output down
.Ed
.It Xo
.Cm workspace
.Op Cm number
.Ar TAG
.Xc
Select a tag on the focused output, or select all tags.
The optional
.Cm number
keyword does not change numeric tag semantics.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+1 workspace number 1
bindsym $mod+0 workspace all
.Ed
.It Cm workspace Ar next|prev|next_on_output|prev_on_output
Select the next or previous output-local tag.
.Cm next_on_output
and
.Cm prev_on_output
are aliases for
.Cm next
and
.Cm prev .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Right workspace next_on_output
bindsym $mod+Left workspace prev_on_output
.Ed
.It Cm workspace back_and_forth
Restore the previous tag view on the focused output.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+BackSpace workspace back_and_forth
.Ed
.It Xo
.Cm move container to workspace
.Op Cm number
.Ar TAG
.Xc
Replace the focused window's tag membership without changing the visible view.
Use
.Cm all
to assign it to every tag on its current output.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+2 move container to workspace number 2
bindsym $mod+Shift+0 move container to workspace all
.Ed
.It Cm move container to workspace Ar next|prev|next_on_output|prev_on_output
Move the focused window to a tag relative to the output's current view.
The command does not select that tag.
Chain a workspace command to follow the window without advancing twice.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+Right move container to workspace next_on_output, workspace next_on_output
bindsym $mod+Shift+Left move container to workspace prev_on_output, workspace prev_on_output
.Ed
.It Cm toggleview Ar TAG
Toggle a tag's visibility instead of replacing the selected tags.
The command does not leave an output with no selected tags.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Control+2 toggleview 2
.Ed
.It Cm toggletag Ar TAG
Toggle a tag in the focused window's membership.
The command does not leave the window with no tags.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Control+Shift+2 toggletag 2
.Ed
.It Cm layout Ar monocle|zones|toggle
Select a layout on the focused output.
The layout names are the same as for the top-level directive.
.Cm toggle
switches between Zones and Monocle.
Layouts are per output, not per tag.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Alt+space layout toggle
.Ed
.It Cm floating Ar on|off|toggle
Set or toggle floating state.
Floating windows keep manual geometry.
This command clears the zone assignment when you turn floating on.
Turn floating off to restore automatic placement.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+space floating toggle
.Ed
.It Cm alwaysOnTop Ar on|off|toggle
Keep the window above ordinary windows on its workspace, but below
fullscreen windows.
This mode is independent of floating.
It suspends zone placement and excludes the window from window switching
and zone cycling. Use the pointer or taskbar to focus it.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+a alwaysOnTop toggle
.Ed
.It Cm fullscreen Ar on|off|toggle
Set or toggle fullscreen state.
Fullscreen covers the output, hides decorations, and retains maximize state
and geometry for restoration.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+f fullscreen toggle
.Ed
.It Cm minimize
Hide the focused window while retaining its tag memberships and taskbar entry.
Taskbar activation or window cycling restores it.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Down minimize
.Ed
.It Cm maximize Ar on|off|toggle
Fill the usable output area, after panel reservations, while keeping decorations.
Turning maximize off restores floating geometry or returns a tiled window to
its layout.
Pointer moving or resizing first removes maximization.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Up maximize toggle
.Ed
.It Cm titlebar Ar on|off|toggle
Set or toggle only the focused window's titlebar.
Floating and fixed-size windows retain their content size.
Tiled frames retain their layout rectangle.
This is separate from the plural
.Cm titlebars
startup policy.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+t titlebar toggle
.Ed
.It Cm border Ar on|off|toggle
Set or toggle the focused window's outer border and titlebar outline.
This does not hide the titlebar itself.
Floating and fixed-size windows retain their content size.
Tiled frames retain their layout rectangle.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+b border toggle
.Ed
.It Cm fixedSize Ar on|off|toggle
Preserve window dimensions during automatic zone placement.
Edge zones align a fixed-size window with the corresponding output edge;
interior zones centre it.
The frame stays within the usable area when its dimensions permit.
Manual mouse movement, snapping, and resizing remain available.
Native fixed-size hints also affect initial placement.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+Shift+f fixedSize toggle
.Ed
.It Cm move container to zone Ar NAME
Assign the focused window to a defined zone and reattach it to automatic layout.
Normal windows fill the zone; fixed-size windows keep their dimensions.
The command does not move fullscreen windows.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+KP_Left move container to zone L
.Ed
.It Cm swap zone with Ar NAME Op Cm focus source
Swap the focused window's zone with the named zone on the same output.
Only windows on visible tags in the Zones layout take part.
Minimized, maximized, fullscreen, and always-on-top windows do not move.
Automatically placed windows fit or align to their new zones.
Windows with manual geometry keep their size and move by the difference
between the two zone origins.
By default, focus follows the focused window into the named zone.
With
.Cm focus source ,
focus the most recently focused window that moved into the original zone.
If no window moved into the original zone, keep focus on the same window.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+F6 swap zone with R
bindsym $mod+F7 swap zone with R focus source
.Ed
.It Cm cycle focus in zone Ar NAME Ns Op | Ns Ar NAME Ns ...
Immediately raise and focus the next eligible member of a zone or zone group
on the focused output's visible tags.
If focus is outside the requested zone or group, raise and focus its
topmost eligible window first.
Use
.Ql |
without surrounding spaces to join names.
Minimized, fullscreen, always-on-top, unassigned, and hidden-tag windows are
excluded. Members with manual geometry can still cycle.
Unlike
.Cm focus next ,
this command does not preview minimized windows or wait for modifier release.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym $mod+KP_Right cycle focus in zone R
bindsym $mod+KP_Add cycle focus in zone L|R
.Ed
.It Cm vt Ar NUMBER
Switch to virtual terminal 1 through 12 on a direct session.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bindsym Ctrl+Alt+F1 vt 1
.Ed
.El
.Ss Complete configuration example
This example replaces the built-in keyboard bindings.
It defines two zones, one startup program, a window rule, and common controls.
Output connector names must match the local system.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
set $mod Mod4
set $term foot
font pango:monospace 10
titlebars on
client.background #222222
client.focused #005577 #005577 #ffffff
client.unfocused #444444
client.urgent #ff0000
fullscreen_background #000000
floating_modifier $mod
layout zones
snap_distance 32
zone L 0% 0% 50% 100%
zone R 50% 0% 50% 100%
output DP-3 {
    default
    position 0 0
    scale 1
}
output HDMI-A-1 position below DP-3 gap 1
for_window [class="^Pianoteq 9$"] fixedSize on
bindsym $mod+Return exec $term
bindsym $mod+w kill
bindsym $mod+Tab focus next
bindsym $mod+Shift+Tab focus prev
bindsym $mod+f fullscreen toggle
bindsym $mod+Down minimize
bindsym $mod+Up maximize toggle
bindsym $mod+Shift+space floating toggle
bindsym $mod+Shift+a alwaysOnTop toggle
bindsym $mod+b border toggle
bindsym $mod+t titlebar toggle
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Alt+space layout toggle
bindsym $mod+Shift+f fixedSize toggle
bindsym $mod+KP_Left move container to zone L
bindsym $mod+KP_Right move container to zone R
bindsym $mod+KP_Add cycle focus in zone L|R
bindsym $mod+1 workspace 1
bindsym $mod+2 workspace 2
bindsym $mod+Shift+2 move container to workspace 2, workspace 2
bindsym $mod+Left workspace prev
bindsym $mod+Right workspace next
bindsym $mod+o focus output next
bindsym $mod+Shift+o swap outputs
bindsym $mod+Shift+r reload
bindsym $mod+Escape exit
exec waybar
.Ed
.Sh TAGS AND ZONES
Tag 1 on one output is separate from tag 1 on another.
Workspace commands select tags on the focused output only.
Layout state is per output, not per tag.
Selected empty tags remain visible in Waybar; unselected empty tags disappear.
Windows with several tag memberships retain every native membership.
.Pp
Floating windows can be zone members without using automatic placement.
Fullscreen and always-on-top windows suspend automatic zone placement.
Reload preserves assignments by name.
If a zone is removed, its members use the first remaining zone.
Removing all custom zones restores the built-in set.
.Pp
With the configured floating modifier, left-drag shows a target-zone preview.
A normal drop attaches the window to the target zone, even if the window
was floating. The layout fits normal windows to the zone and aligns fixed-size
windows without changing their size.
Hold Shift in addition to the floating modifier when pressing the left button
to keep the drop position and size. This alternate drag is available when
.Cm floating_modifier
does not already include Shift. Edge snapping still applies during the drag.
For a normal drop, zones under the pointer take priority over zones that only
overlap the window. Among those zones, greatest overlap wins; size similarity
resolves ties, then declaration order.
When the pointer is outside all zones, normal drops use the overlap ranking
without the pointer preference. With no overlap, the nearest zone centre wins.
Cross-output drops use the destination's selected tags.
.Pp
Right-drag resizes the corner nearest the pointer when the drag starts.
The pointer moves to that corner, the opposite corner stays fixed, and
snapping applies to the dragged edges.
During a Shift-drag or resize, the preview has half the opacity of the normal
preview. It shows the zone on the destination output that overlaps the
largest area of the window frame. The current zone wins a tie. If no zone
overlaps the frame, there is no preview or change in assignment.
On release, the window keeps its position and size and does not use automatic
placement. Use an explicit zone command or turn floating off to restore
automatic placement.
Left-drag a titlebar to move a window without a modifier.
.Pp
In zones mode, floating, fixed-size, maximized, and zone windows share normal
focus-based stacking; mouse resizing does not make a window always-on-top.
Fullscreen windows and their dialogs keep their separate layer.
Monocle keeps floating windows above layout-managed windows.
.Pp
When
.Cm window_stack_picker
is enabled, a badge at the upper-right corner of a window counts the front
window and the eligible windows it covers in the same stacking layer.
The windows must share a zone or a corresponding frame corner.
The front window must cover at least 25% of the other window's frame
inside the front window's zone.
The badge only appears on a visible titlebar.
Click the badge to list the front window first, then the covered windows.
The list uses installed PNG app icons when available and a window icon otherwise.
Click a row, or use the arrow keys and Enter, to bring a window forward without
moving or resizing it.
Use the mouse wheel to browse a long list.
Press Escape or click outside the list to close it.
.Pp
The built-in bindings below apply only when no configuration file exists.
.Sh KEY BINDINGS
The default modifier is Super (the Windows key).
Super-Shift-Return starts foot. Super-p starts bemenu-run.
Super-j and Super-k change focus.
Super-b toggles the border and Super-t toggles the titlebar.
Super-Ctrl-Alt-Space switches between Zones and Monocle.
Super-Shift-Space toggles floating for the focused window.
Super-Shift-a toggles always-on-top for the focused window.
Super-e toggles fullscreen. Super-Shift-c and Super-w request a polite close.
Super-1 through Super-9 select tags. Add Shift to assign a client to a tag.
Add Control to toggle a tag instead of replacing the tag selection.
Super-0 selects all tags. Super-Tab selects the previous tag view.
Super-comma and Super-period change output. Add Shift to move a client to that output.
Super-Shift-q exits.
.Sh POINTER BINDINGS
Super-left drag moves a client. Super-Shift-left drag keeps the drop geometry.
Super-right drag resizes the nearest corner. Adding Shift does not change
the resize behavior.
Super-middle toggles floating.
.Pp
Hold Super and scroll up or down to select the previous or next workspace
on the output under the pointer. Add Shift to move the window under the pointer
to that workspace, switch the view to it, and focus the moved window.
Both shortcuts wrap between workspaces 1 and 9 and include empty workspaces.
These shortcuts use
.Cm floating_modifier .
Setting it to
.Cm none
disables the scroll shortcuts. If it already includes Shift, the
window-moving scroll shortcut is unavailable.
.Sh STATUS
Status lines contain an output name, a component name, and its value.
Components are title, appid, fullscreen, floating, selmon, tags, and layout.
The tags component contains occupied, selected, focused-client, and urgent masks.
Nimzo does not print this status stream by default.
With
.Fl s ,
Nimzo sends it to the startup command's standard input.
That command must read this stream or close its standard input.
.Sh WAYLAND CAPABILITIES
With
.Cm wayland_protocols_restricted on
(the default), clients on the default Wayland socket cannot bind privileged
globals.
Set
.Cm wayland_protocols_restricted off
to let all Wayland clients bind them without a profile.
Restart Nimzo to change this setting; reloading does not change the Wayland
registry policy.
Nimzo passes an already connected socket to a profile process with
.Ev WAYLAND_SOCKET .
When restrictions are on, only that connection gets the profile's capabilities:
.Bl -tag -width output-control
.It Cm capture
Screen, output, and window capture.
To take a screenshot with
.Xr grim 1 ,
configure a capture profile and launch it from a binding:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
wayland_client screenshot capture /usr/bin/grim
bindsym Print launch screenshot
.Ed
Use
.Fl t Ar jpeg
and an output path in the profile to save a JPEG.
When restrictions are on, starting
.Ic grim
directly from a terminal does not grant capture access.
.It Cm clipboard
Clipboard data control without normal seat focus.
.It Cm virtual-input
Virtual keyboards and pointers.
.It Cm input-method
Input-method registration.
.It Cm session-lock
Session locking.
.It Cm toplevel
Listing and controlling other windows.
.It Cm output-control
Output configuration, power, and gamma control.
.El
.Pp
A reload changes grants for new launches only.
Close a connection to revoke its grants.
Choose executables and configurations you trust: a process can pass its
connection to another process.
This is not a sandbox for processes running as the same user.
The default socket still supports ordinary applications and layer-shell clients.
The separate
.Ev SWAYSOCK
permits shell commands and configured profile launches unless restricted.
Do not pass the unrestricted socket to untrusted clients.
.Sh WAYBAR AND IPC
XDG output metadata and layer shell support bars on each enabled output.
An exclusive panel reduces that output's usable area.
Omit Waybar's output filter to create a bar on every output.
See examples/waybar.jsonc and examples/waybar.css.
.Pp
The SWAYSOCK endpoint supports I3/Sway framing, state queries, subscriptions,
and commands through existing compositor actions.
Workspace names are OUTPUT:TAG; plain numbers select tags on the focused output.
The IPC workspace number is -1 so bar clicks retain the output-qualified name.
The example uses sway/workspaces, sway/window, wlr/taskbar, and clock.
Taskbar activation and close use foreign-toplevel management.
Minimized windows retain taskbar entries; activation restores them.
IPC does not implement the full Sway command language.
.Pp
By default,
.Cm sway_ipc_restricted on
limits this socket to workspace and tree queries, workspace and window
subscriptions, and workspace selection.
Other requests, including configuration queries, are rejected.
The restriction applies to each command in a chain.
Set
.Cm sway_ipc_restricted off
to allow the other supported requests and commands.
Reload the configuration from a key binding to change this setting.
Taskbar actions use a separate Wayland connection and are not affected.
.Pp
Commas, semicolons, and newlines separate IPC commands outside quotes.
Exec consumes the remainder as shell text.
Commands are rejected while the session is locked.
.Pp
Use swaymsg from a terminal started within the nimzo session.
The socket is private and is removed at shutdown.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
A private runtime directory, normally set by the session manager.
.It Ev SWAYSOCK , I3SOCK
Set by nimzo to its Unix IPC socket before startup programs run.
.It Ev WLR_BACKENDS
Select a wlroots backend, for example wayland or headless.
.El
